# ----------------------------------------------------------------------------
/gps/source/list
# ----------------------------------------------------------------------------

# /run/beamOn 10000000


# ============================================================================
#  NORMALIZATION -- from reactor power (unambiguous, use this)
# ============================================================================
#  Fissions per second:   P / (200 MeV)  =  P[W] / 3.204e-11 J
#  Source neutrons:       x nu-bar,  nu = 2.4367 for U-235 thermal
#                                    (prompt 2.4200 + delayed 0.0167)
#
#      P = 12 kW   ->  3.745e14 fiss/s  ->  S = 9.13e14 n/s
#      P = 10 kW   ->  3.121e14 fiss/s  ->  S = 7.61e14 n/s
#      P =  1 kW   ->  3.121e13 fiss/s  ->  S = 7.61e13 n/s
#
#  The 200 MeV/fission convention carries about +/-3%; it is the recoverable
#  energy including delayed betas and gammas, which is the right convention
#  to pair with a measured steady-state thermal power.
#
#  Per-primary weight for N simulated histories:
#      w = S / N          [n/s per primary]
#      N = 1e7, P = 12 kW  ->  w = 9.13e7 n/s per primary
#
#  Any tally scales as:  rate = (tally sum / N) * S
#
#  You do NOT need the fuel meat volume. GPS with confine samples uniformly
#  over the meat, so the spatial density is already correct; S is a pure
#  scalar multiplier applied afterwards.
#
#  CROSS-CHECK from your 3e10 flux (optional):
#      R_f = N_235 * sigma_f_bar * PHI_th      [fissions/cm3/s]
#      sigma_f(0.0253 eV) = 585 b
#      sigma_f_bar ~ 505 b for a 293.6 K Maxwellian FLUX spectrum
#                   (= (2/sqrt(pi)) * g_f * sigma_0, g_f = 0.976)
#  Beware the convention: whether PHI_th means the 2200 m/s-equivalent flux
#  or the integral of the Maxwellian changes this by 2/sqrt(pi) = 0.886.
#  Multiply R_f by the meat volume and by nu to get S, and check it against
#  the power-based number above. If they disagree by more than ~30% your
#  flux figure and your power figure are not describing the same state.
#
#  FLAT SOURCE ASSUMPTION: uniform sampling in the meat means a flat fission
#  density. A real MTR core runs peak-to-average ~1.3-1.5 axially (cosine-ish)
#  and radially, modified upward at the edges by the graphite reflector. GPS
#  cannot impose an arbitrary 3D shape in one source; if it matters, split
#  into several sources with /gps/source/add, each confined to a different
#  axial slice or assembly group, with intensities following the shape.
#
# ============================================================================
#  DOUBLE-COUNTING WARNING -- read this if your geometry contains the fuel
# ============================================================================
#  At steady state the total fission neutron birth rate S already accounts
#  for every generation: every generation is the same size. So emitting S
#  Watt neutrons and transporting them ONE generation gives the correct
#  steady-state flux everywhere.
#
#  If you leave fissile material in the transport geometry with _HP physics,
#  Geant4 will induce further fissions and multiply your source. You then get
#  flux ~ S/(1-k), which for PUR-1 (k ~ 1) is unbounded and numerically
#  meaningless -- the answer will depend entirely on how close your modelled
#  k lands to unity. Suppress induced-fission neutrons in a StackingAction:
#
#      G4ClassificationOfNewTrack
#      MyStackingAction::ClassifyNewTrack(const G4Track* trk)
#      {
#        if (trk->GetParentID() == 0) return fUrgent;         // primaries
#        const G4VProcess* cr = trk->GetCreatorProcess();
#        if (trk->GetDefinition() == G4Neutron::Definition()
#            && cr && cr->GetProcessName() == "nFission") return fKill;
#        return fUrgent;
#      }
#
#  Kill only the NEUTRONS, not the whole nFission secondary list -- the
#  prompt fission gammas are real field that your neutron-only source does
#  not supply, and you may want them for the gamma background.
#
#  If your geometry is moderator + detector only, with no fuel, none of this
#  applies and the macro is complete as written.
#
# ============================================================================
#  OTHER THINGS THAT WILL BITE
# ============================================================================
#  1. DELAYED NEUTRONS. 0.0167/2.4367 = 0.69% of the source, mean energy
#     ~0.4-0.5 MeV instead of 2.03 MeV. Negligible for a prompt-response
#     detector; matters only for kinetics or a pulsed-source measurement.
#     If you need it, add a second GPS source at intensity 0.0069 with a
#     Maxwellian at T = 0.30 MeV.
#
#  2. STATISTICS THROUGH THE POOL. If your detector sits any real distance
#     into 5.2 m of water, an analogue run will deliver essentially zero
#     tallies -- each mean free path costs you an order of magnitude.
#     Geant4 has no weight windows. Either keep the detector near the core,
#     or do it in two steps: run once to score a surface current on a shell
#     around the core, then use that as an Arb-tabulated surface source for
#     the second stage.
#
#  3. THERMAL SCATTERING. You are starting at 2 MeV and moderating down to
#     0.0253 eV, so the last few decades depend entirely on having
#     S(alpha,beta) enabled -- use an _HPT physics list variant or add
#     G4NeutronHPThermalScattering explicitly, and register the thermal
#     scattering material names for your water and your polymer. Without it
#     the free-gas approximation puts your B-10 capture rate off by a
#     noticeable factor.
#
#  4. VALIDATION. beamOn 1e5 and histogram primary KE before scoring:
#     peak at 0.74 MeV, mean 2.03 MeV, and 40.6% of primaries above 2 MeV.
#     If the peak lands at 0.74 eV or 0.74 GeV, the /gps/hist/point unit
#     assumption is wrong for your Geant4 version -- fix it before anything
#     else. Also verify /gps/source/list reports the confine volume as
#     found; a typo'd volume name silently gives you an unconfined box.
# ============================================================================
